<div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose">
<h2>How an insurance appraisal works in New York</h2>
<p>Appraisal is a short, contract-based procedure for one narrow problem: you and your insurer agree that a property loss is covered, at least in part, but you cannot agree on what it costs to put right. Instead of litigating the number, each side names an appraiser, the two appraisers choose an umpire, and an award signed by any two of the three sets the amount of loss. The eight steps below describe how that usually plays out for homeowners, co-op and condo owners and small commercial owners across New York State, from a Buffalo double to a Manhattan pre-war apartment.</p>
<p>Every policy is worded a little differently, so treat this as a general walkthrough. Read your own appraisal clause alongside it, and talk to a New York attorney about any question of coverage, deadlines or legal rights.</p>
<div class="note"><b class="h">Where the clause comes from</b>The appraisal wording in most U.S. property policies traces back to the 1943 New York Standard Fire Policy, which is still set out in New York Insurance Law §3404. Its basic design (a written demand, two competent and disinterested appraisers, an umpire, and an award by any two of the three) has been copied into homeowners and commercial forms nationwide, so New York policyholders are working with the original model.</div>

<h2 id="step-1">Step 1: Confirm the dispute is about amount</h2>
<p>Appraisal is built to settle disagreements over <strong>how much</strong>, not <strong>whether</strong>. If the carrier accepted the claim but priced a roof replacement at a fraction of your contractor’s bid, that is an amount dispute. If the carrier denied the claim because it says the damage was caused by long-term seepage rather than a sudden pipe burst, that is primarily a coverage dispute, and appraisal may not be the right tool.</p>
<p>Many real claims mix both. A carrier may accept wind damage to one slope of a Long Island cape while refusing the other three. The panel can usually value each item, but it generally cannot rule on whether an item is covered. Before demanding appraisal, list each disputed item and mark whether the disagreement is about price, quantity or scope, or about coverage.</p>
<div class="note"><b class="h">What appraisal can’t decide</b>An appraisal panel generally does not decide coverage, interpret disputed policy language, award interest or attorney’s fees, or rule on claim-handling complaints under Insurance Law §2601 or Regulation 64. Those questions belong with the New York State Department of Financial Services or a New York attorney.</div>

<h2 id="step-2">Step 2: Gather the claim file</h2>
<p>A well-organized file makes the rest of the process faster and cheaper. Before you demand appraisal, assemble:</p>
<ul>
<li>The full policy, including the declarations page and every endorsement</li>
<li>The carrier’s estimate, any revised estimates and every payment letter</li>
<li>Your contractor’s estimate or an independent estimate, ideally in line-item form</li>
<li>Dated photos and video of the damage, including before-and-after mitigation shots</li>
<li>Invoices for emergency repairs, water extraction, tarping or board-up</li>
<li>Correspondence with the adjuster, including any reservation-of-rights letter</li>
</ul>
<p>Co-op and condo owners should also request the board’s or managing agent’s information about the building’s master policy, because the line between building and unit coverage often drives the numbers.</p>

<h2 id="step-3">Step 3: Send the written demand</h2>
<p>The New York standard form, and most policies modeled on it, requires the demand to be in writing. A clear demand identifies the policy and claim numbers, states that the parties disagree on the amount of loss, invokes the appraisal provision, and names your appraiser or commits to naming one within the policy’s time frame.</p>
<p>Send it in a way you can prove later, such as email plus certified mail. Check whether your policy asks for a sworn proof of loss or other conditions first. Keep the suit-limitation clause in view as well: many New York property policies limit lawsuits to two years from the date of loss, and appraisal does not necessarily pause that clock. Check yours, and ask a New York attorney if the date is close.</p>

<h2 id="step-4">Step 4: Each side names an appraiser</h2>
<p>Each party selects its own appraiser. The statutory New York form gives each side 20 days from the demand to choose one and notify the other, and many policies use similar wording. The word that matters most is <em>disinterested</em>: your appraiser should have no financial stake in the outcome beyond a fee, and some carriers challenge appraisers paid on a percentage of the award. Ask how any appraiser you hire is paid and get the arrangement in writing.</p>
<p>Your appraiser is retained by you and advocates for a well-supported number, but should still be able to defend every line item in front of a neutral umpire.</p>

<h2 id="step-5">Step 5: The appraisers select an umpire</h2>
<p>The two appraisers then try to agree on a competent and disinterested umpire. Under the standard form, if they cannot agree within 15 days, either party can ask a judge of a court of record in the state to appoint one. In New York that request usually goes to the Supreme Court in the county where the property sits, typically with the help of an attorney.</p>
<p>Good umpires are often chosen early, even if they are never needed, so the panel does not stall later. Umpires for New York losses are frequently experienced contractors, estimators, engineers or retired adjusters familiar with local pricing in places like Westchester or the Capital Region.</p>

<h2 id="step-6">Step 6: Inspection and estimate exchange</h2>
<p>Each appraiser inspects the property, ideally together, and builds or refines a line-item estimate. For a roof, that means measuring squares and pitch, confirming the number of existing layers and checking flashing and ice-and-water shield. For a water loss in a brownstone, it might mean tracing moisture through plaster walls and documenting original trim that affects matching.</p>
<h3>What gets compared</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Scope:</strong> which rooms, surfaces and components need work</li>
<li><strong>Quantity:</strong> square feet, linear feet, roofing squares, fixtures</li>
<li><strong>Pricing:</strong> local unit costs, often in Xactimate, plus overhead and profit</li>
<li><strong>Depreciation:</strong> how much is withheld for age and condition</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="step-7">Step 7: Negotiation and umpire review</h2>
<p>Most of the work happens between the two appraisers. They walk through the estimates line by line, agree what they can, and narrow the gap. Many appraisals end here, with both appraisers signing an agreed award and no umpire decision at all.</p>
<p>Items they cannot agree on go to the umpire. Each appraiser submits a position with supporting evidence: photos, measurements, supplier quotes, code citations or manufacturer installation instructions. The umpire may inspect the property, ask questions or meet with both appraisers, then decide each open item. The process is less formal than a trial; there is usually no testimony under oath and no strict rules of evidence.</p>

<h2 id="step-8">Step 8: Award and payment</h2>
<p>The award is a written document signed by at least two of the three panel members. Under the standard form, that award determines the amount of loss. A clear award separates replacement cost value, depreciation and actual cash value, and splits items by coverage part, such as dwelling, other structures, personal property and loss of use, so the carrier can apply the policy correctly.</p>
<div class="note"><b class="h">Illustrative example</b>Illustrative example: an award for a dwelling loss might read replacement cost value $84,000, less depreciation of $21,000, for an actual cash value of $63,000. If the deductible is $2,500 and the carrier already paid $30,000, the next ACV payment would be $30,500. Of the $21,000 depreciation, the portion the policy treats as recoverable would generally be paid once repairs are completed and documented. These figures are invented to show the arithmetic only.</div>
<p>After the award, the carrier applies deductibles, prior payments and policy limits, then issues payment under the policy’s terms. If a coverage question remains open, the carrier may pay the undisputed portion and reserve its position on the rest, which is a conversation for a New York attorney.</p>

<h2>Timelines and costs</h2>
<p>There is no single schedule. A straightforward residential appraisal where both appraisers engage promptly can wrap up in weeks; a large commercial loss, a co-op building claim or a panel that needs an umpire hearing can take months. Delays most often come from slow appraiser appointments, missing documentation, or arguments over whether an item is a coverage question.</p>
<p>On cost, each side generally pays its own appraiser and shares the umpire’s fee and other appraisal expenses equally, as the standard form provides. Appraiser fees may be hourly, flat or structured in other ways; ask for the arrangement in writing before you sign. On a small gap, fees can absorb much of any increase, so test the numbers first with our <a href="/appraisal-calculators/settlement-gap-fee-estimator/">settlement gap and fee estimator</a>, or <a href="/reach-us/">contact us</a> for a no-obligation review of your carrier’s estimate.</p>
</article>
